## Sentiment target limitation clarified, 24 November 2026

The user found "Whole-sentence sentiment can concern someone other than the named player" unclear and asked for elaboration if retained. The relevant project implementation is that RoBERTa receives the selected sentence alone, without a separate input identifying the player whose portrayal is being measured; player-name matching selects sentences but does not make the classifier target-specific. The human validation judges sentiment toward the named player. This distinction explains a concrete possible measurement error, not an established age bias or quantified error rate for the full corpus.

Proposed replacement in [discussion.tex](../discussion.tex): "RoBERTa scores the whole sentence without being told which player to evaluate. Its score may therefore reflect praise or criticism of another person mentioned in the same sentence." The Hamborg–Donnay citation remains attached to the general target-dependent sentiment concern; the [project measurement review](../evidence_and_sources.md#from-sentences-to-signing-period-tone) and [scorer](../../data/dataset_nlp/sentiment/a1_sentiment.py) establish the actual input design. [Verified NewsMTSC role](issue07_literature_review.md#guardian-measurement-and-provenance) concerns measurement design in news, not evidence that this specific model misclassified a particular project sentence.

Illustrative explanation only: "Smith watched as Jones delivered an outstanding performance." This invented sentence names Smith but praises Jones; a positive whole-sentence score could therefore be attributed to Smith's coverage without evaluating Smith positively. Do not put this example forward as a retrieved Guardian sentence, observed model prediction or new empirical result. No scoring or corpus reconstruction was performed, and original sentences remain unavailable at documented paths.

