## Empirical p-value wording aligned with course, 24 November 2026

The user questioned framing the test as `p=(1+b)/1000`, referring to `1/(shuffles+1)` in the saved course HTML. Read the local [Week 2 HTML](<../../../data/week2/Week 2 · Models & null models — 02805.html>) directly, in the "Shuffling is a permutation test" section (HTML line 589). Its formula is `(1 + number of shuffles at least as extreme)/(1 + number of shuffles)`. The separate `1/(B+1)` expression is the smallest attainable value of this rule, when no shuffle is equally or more extreme; it is not the general empirical p-value for a nonzero tail count.

The [H1 implementation](../../notebooks/age_shuffle_outsideprem_toprem.py), `_lower_tail_p_value()`, uses `(1 + lower_or_equal)/(1 + number of shuffled gaps)`, with `<=` so ties count. The [saved restricted results](../../reruns/h1_2012_13-2024_25/output/age_shuffle_outsideprem_toprem_results.json) record 999 shuffles and 97 lower-or-equal draws for main H1, giving the already approved 98/1000 = 0.098; the fixed-player result has 73 such draws and 74/1000 = 0.074. This was a read-only source comparison, not a rerun or new analysis.

Replaced only the proposed p-value sentence in [methods.tex](../methods.tex) with: "The empirical one-sided p-value was the fraction of shuffled gaps at or below the observed gap, with one added to both the numerator and denominator." The established Hemerik–Goeman/Phipson–Smyth citations remain. This uses the course's empirical framing without a hard-coded denominator or undefined-letter burden. The lower-tail test, inclusive ties, plus-one correction and numerical findings are unchanged. The replacement and rest of H1 Methods remain under review.

Updated issue/README/tracker and checked tracked-file whitespace with `git diff --check`. Exact sample text and approved Results/Discussion/earlier Methods, figures/table/captions remain preserved. No scripts producing empirical results, scoring, environment change, staging, commit or push. Next: review the empirical p-value explanation and H1 Methods, then draft Guardian article/coverage Methods. Issue 12 Open, contributions deferred and Issue 9.1 deferred/nonblocking.

## Empirical p-value equation added, 24 November 2026

The user agreed to the empirical p-value explanation and explicitly requested adding its mathematical formula to the report. Preserved the agreed plain-language sentence, replacing its final full stop with a colon, and added a native LaTeX `equation` in [methods.tex](../methods.tex), labelled `eq:h1-empirical-p`:

`p_emp = (1 + number of shuffles at least as extreme)/(1 + number of shuffles)`.

This is the general formula shown in the course-alignment explanation, rather than the special-case minimum `1/(B+1)` or a hard-coded denominator. The preceding H1 sentence defines "at least as extreme" as shuffled gaps at or below the observed gap, including ties. No new symbols or separate assumptions are introduced. The Hemerik–Goeman and Phipson–Smyth citations remain attached to the calculation. It is the same implemented rule and leaves p = 0.098 / 0.074 unchanged.

